SubPixR — Licensing
======================================================================

Copyright 2026 The SubPixR Authors
https://github.com/asergiu-ubb/SubPixR

This repository is released under more than one set of terms, because the code
and the trained model carry different obligations. In short:

  SOURCE CODE            MIT License (Part A)
                         Commercial use permitted.

  MODEL WEIGHTS          PolyForm Noncommercial License 1.0.0 (Part B)
                         Noncommercial research, teaching and personal use
                         only. Commercial use is NOT permitted.

  TWO EVALUATION FILES   Apache License 2.0, Copyright Google LLC (Part C)
                         Not ours to relicense; their headers govern.

Why the model is more restricted than the code: SubPixR was trained on
synthetic images rendered from the Replica dataset and from Google Scanned
Objects. The Replica Research Terms permit use "only in connection with a
research or educational purpose that is non-commercial or not-for-profit in
nature", and that restriction carries over to derivative works, including
models trained on renders of the data. We therefore cannot grant commercial
rights in the weights, and no one downstream can either. The source code
contains none of those assets and is unencumbered.


SCOPE — which terms apply to which files
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Part A (MIT) covers every file in this repository EXCEPT those listed under
Parts B and C:

    subpixr/, data/, eval/ (except the two files in Part C),
    refiners_compared/, scripts/, train.py, requirements.txt, README.md

Part B (PolyForm Noncommercial 1.0.0) covers the released trained model:

    checkpoints/best_model.pth
    checkpoints/config.json
    any weights derived from them by fine-tuning or conversion

Part C (Apache 2.0, Google LLC) covers:

    eval/tapvid_metrics.py
    eval/transforms.py

A note on datasets you render yourself: data/generate_synthetic_dataset.py is
MIT-licensed under Part A, but the Replica scenes and Google Scanned Objects
assets it reads are not ours, and any dataset you render from them is governed
directly by the terms in Part D — not by this file.


